 "news": {
  "date": "2026-10-08",
  "asof": "2026-10-09 11:39 UTC",
  "headline": "The AI trade cracked on its own \u2014 and the rest of the market did not notice",
  "standfirst": "The Nasdaq fell 1.25% on a semiconductor sell-off, after an Nvidia-backed Australian data-centre company failed to raise the support it had sought and Ray Dalio restated his AI bubble warning \u2014 while the Dow rose 0.10%, the Russell 0.03% and most S&P 500 stocks closed higher. A sector event inside a market that stayed still. The long end eased for the second time in three sessions, the ten-year reaching toward 5.35% and closing near 5.24% through a thirty-year auction that was the first direct test of the supply argument. Jobless claims came in at 197,000 against 200,000 expected, near a 57-year low for a fourth week \u2014 six days after payrolls printed 29,000. And Trump ruled out a strike on Iran before the November midterms, though tankers were attacked in the Strait anyway and crude rose to $91.49 on Hurricane Isaias. In the book GOOG was the only faller, down 0.72% after touching 353.53, sold with the complex it funds while the names selling into it rose; AAPL rose 1.11% to 340.42 and closed above the level it held before the Ternus restructuring, fully recovering the repricing after eight sessions, while OKTA made a new closing high at +1.86R. Heat 3.05R against 3.0R, unchanged for a twenty-first session.",
  "themes": [
   {
    "kicker": "What's next",
    "right": "CPI Tuesday \u00b7 then 28 October",
    "title": "Everything now rides on one print",
    "items": [
     {
      "tone": "c-cb",
      "tag": "CPI",
      "html": "<b>September CPI</b> \u2014 the only first-tier print before the Fed, and the tie-break between the minutes and the data",
      "when": "Tue \u00b7 2026-10-13",
      "kind": "calendar"
     },
     {
      "tone": "c-fomc",
      "tag": "FOMC",
      "html": "<b>Fed decision</b> \u2014 the minutes promise another hike; the market prices a 78% hold",
      "when": "Wed \u00b7 2026-10-28",
      "kind": "calendar"
     },
     {
      "tone": "c-cb",
      "tag": "GOOG",
      "html": "<b>Alphabet Q3 earnings</b> \u2014 $195-205bn of capex and 3.6GW of contracted power, into an AI funding scare",
      "when": "Wed \u00b7 2026-10-28",
      "kind": "calendar"
     },
     {
      "tone": "c-cb",
      "tag": "AAPL",
      "html": "<b>Apple Q4 earnings</b> \u2014 the first iPhone 18 quarter and the first read on Ternus",
      "when": "Thu \u00b7 2026-10-29",
      "kind": "calendar"
     }
    ],
    "note": "<b>September CPI on Tuesday now has to settle three arguments at once.</b> Whether the two ISM prices indices at <b>77.9</b> and <b>74.0</b> are feeding through or stalling at the factory gate. Whether the Fed's minutes and Waller's comments describe a committee that will actually hike or one that is talking tough. And whether the long end's behaviour is an inflation trade or a supply one \u2014 because Thursday's auction was the first evidence for the second reading and a hot CPI would wash it away."
   }
  ],
  "ondeck": "<b>September CPI on Tuesday now has to settle three arguments at once.</b> Whether the two ISM prices indices at <b>77.9</b> and <b>74.0</b> are feeding through or stalling at the factory gate. Whether the Fed's minutes and Waller's comments describe a committee that will actually hike or one that is talking tough. And whether the long end's behaviour is an inflation trade or a supply one \u2014 because Thursday's auction was the first evidence for the second reading and a hot CPI would wash it away.",
